# {{ package_name }}

{{ package_description }}

A React frontend and a FastAPI backend in one repository, agreeing on a generated
OpenAPI contract.

## Quickstart

```bash
make install        # the git hooks, both halves, and the browser
make dev            # backend on :8000, frontend on :5173
```

The store is in memory until you set `DATABASE_URL`, so that works on a clean machine with
no database running. Point it at Postgres and the app applies its own schema on startup:

```bash
make db              # starts one and prints the DATABASE_URL to export
```

Or run the frontend alone, with no backend and no Python at all:

```bash
make dev-frontend   # mock mode: the handlers in frontend/src/mocks answer
```

## Layout

```
frontend/     React 19, Vite, TanStack Router + Query, Tailwind v4, MSW
backend/      FastAPI, pydantic v2, uvicorn, asyncpg
openapi.json  the contract -- generated from the backend, consumed by the frontend
deploy/       schema.sql -- generated from backend/app/store/ddl.py
```

Each half installs, lints, tests and builds on its own. `pnpm` runs in `frontend/`,
`uv` in `backend/`, and `make` at the root fans out to both.

## The contract

The backend is the source of truth. After changing a model or a route:

```bash
make openapi        # regenerates openapi.json, frontend/src/api/schema.ts and parts.ts
```

Commit both alongside the change; `make openapi-check` fails if they drift. The frontend's
mock handlers are typed against the spec, so a mock that disagrees with the backend is a
build error rather than a surprise at runtime — and `make test-contract` runs one test suite
against both implementations to prove they still agree.

## Everyday commands

```bash
make pre-commit     # static checks and commit tests, once per tree
make suite          # full suite, live contracts and frontend build
make lint           # both halves
make test           # both halves, then the contract suite across both
make test-e2e       # opt-in: Playwright in mock mode, in a real browser
make db-test        # opt-in: backend/tests/integration/, on a Postgres it starts itself
make build          # production build of the frontend
make upgrade        # update dependencies, then regenerate the contract
```

The git hook runs `make gate-commit` through `make pre-commit`. `make suite` runs the full
check and builds the frontend. Neither needs a browser or a database daemon.
{% if github_ci -%}
The GitHub workflow runs `make suite` on pull requests and pushes. It runs `make db-test`
in a separate job. The other opt-in tiers need a caller of their own.
{%- else -%}
Configure your CI or release build to run `make suite`. It must stop promotion when that
check fails or has no result. The opt-in tiers need callers of their own.
{%- endif %}

